[E] N1: finite-T decay => floor, WITH the exact rate; T=inf is the gap
==============================================================================

  Lemma (T).  nu>=0, C(t)=int e^{-s t} dnu, M0=C(0). Suppose
        C(t) <= M0 (1+theta) e^{-c t}      for all t in [0,T].
  Then for every eps in [0,c):
        nu([0,eps))  <=  M0 (1+theta) exp(-(c-eps) T) .
  Proof: C(t) >= nu([0,eps)) e^{-eps t}, plug t=T.
  Corollary: T<inf gives nu([0,eps)) <= eta only for
        T >= log(M0(1+theta)/eta)/(c-eps) ,
  and nu([0,eps))=0 (a floor at eps) requires T=inf. So the "rate" one needs is
  theta(T), the DEFECT of the decay bound -- and theta==0 (all T) IS the gap.

    eta=   1e-02  eps=0.0  c=1.0  ->  T >=    4.605
    eta=   1e-02  eps=0.5  c=1.0  ->  T >=    9.210
    eta=   1e-04  eps=0.0  c=1.0  ->  T >=    9.210
    eta=   1e-04  eps=0.5  c=1.0  ->  T >=   18.421
    eta=   1e-06  eps=0.0  c=1.0  ->  T >=   13.816
    eta=   1e-06  eps=0.5  c=1.0  ->  T >=   27.631
    eta=   1e-10  eps=0.0  c=1.0  ->  T >=   23.026
    eta=   1e-10  eps=0.5  c=1.0  ->  T >=   46.052
    -> T ~ (1/(c-eps)) log(1/eta):  divergence as eps->0 (the floor is
       the eps=0 endpoint) and as eta->0 (exactness).  No finite T.

